"Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Italian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Budino is a sweet Italian dessert, usually rich and creamy like a custard or pudding. Like the English word 'pudding', budino originally referred to a type of medieval sausage. It can be thickened with cornstarch or cookies to make it more like a soufflé or ganache, and it can be sauced with various flavors, including chocolate, caramel, apple, and butterscotch."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-budino"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Budino"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pasta Grannies","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pastagrannies.substack.com","note":"Vicky Bennison's long-running video series filming Italian grandmothers making regional pasta by hand.","url":"https://pastagrannies.substack.com/p/pasta-grannies-is-now-on-substack","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Recipes from Italy","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"recipesfromitaly.com","note":"Traditional Italian recipes written by Italian home cooks, organized by region and course.","url":"https://recipesfromitaly.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Manu's Menu","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"manusmenu.com","note":"Italian-born home cook Manuela sharing classic family and regional Italian dishes.","url":"https://www.manusmenu.com/about/about-this-site","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Italian cuisine","links":[["Acquacotta","dish/acquacotta"],["Agliata","dish/agliata"],["Agnolotti","dish/agnolotti"],["Antipasto","dish/antipasto"],["Arancini","dish/arancini"],["Biscotti","dish/biscotti"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Bombolone","dish/bombolone"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Cannelloni","dish/cannelloni"],["Capellini","dish/capellini"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Carpaccio","dish/carpaccio"],["Cassatella di sant'Agata","dish/cassatella-di-sant-agata"],["Cassoeula","dish/cassoeula"],["Ciabatta","dish/ciabatta"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Coda alla vaccinara","dish/coda-alla-vaccinara"],["Colomba pasquale","dish/colomba-pasquale"],["Conti roll","dish/conti-roll"],["Cotoletta alla bolognese","dish/cotoletta-alla-bolognese"],["Crostata","dish/crostata"],["Drago restaurants","dish/drago-restaurants"],["Focaccia pugliese","dish/focaccia-pugliese"],["Frittata","dish/frittata"],["Giardiniera","dish/giardiniera"],["Guanciale","dish/guanciale"],["Italian restaurant","dish/italian-restaurant"],["Italian wedding soup","dish/italian-wedding-soup"],["Lampredotto","dish/lampredotto"],["Lasagna","dish/lasagna"],["List of Italian restaurants","dish/list-of-italian-restaurants"],["Marinara sauce","dish/marinara-sauce"],["Mondeghili","dish/mondeghili"],["Paccheri","dish/paccheri"],["Panini (sandwich)","dish/panini-sandwich"],["Panna cotta","dish/panna-cotta"],["Passatelli","dish/passatelli"],["Pecorino","dish/pecorino"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/1a/Griesmeelpudding_met_rode_bessen_saus.jpg/500px-Griesmeelpudding_met_rode_bessen_saus.jpg","w":500,"h":513,"by":"Takeaway","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Griesmeelpudding_met_rode_bessen_saus.jpg"}},"ingredient/holy-basil":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/holy-basil","n":"Holy basil","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 7 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Ocimum tenuiflorum, commonly known as tulasi, tulsi, or holy basil, is an aromatic perennial plant in the family Lamiaceae. It is widely cultivated throughout the Southeast Asian tropics. It is native to tropical and subtropical regions of Asia, Australia and the western Pacific. This plant has  escaped from cultivation and has naturalized in many tropical regions of the Americas. It is an agricultural and environmental weed."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-holy-basil"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holy_basil"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Karedok","dish/karedok"],["Laksa","dish/laksa"],["Nasi tempong","dish/nasi-tempong"],["Nasi ulam","dish/nasi-ulam"],["Tinutuan","dish/tinutuan"]]],["Cambodian cuisine","cuisine/cambodian-cuisine",[["Chha kh'nhei","dish/chha-kh-nhei"]]],["Lao cuisine","cuisine/lao-cuisine",[["Drunken noodles","dish/drunken-noodles"]]],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine",[["Drunken noodles","dish/drunken-noodles"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Kencur","ingredient/kencur"],["Peanut sauce","ingredient/peanut-sauce"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Ginger root","ingredient/ginger-root"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Baby corn","ingredient/baby-corn"],["Bean sprout","ingredient/bean-sprout"],["Fish sauce","ingredient/fish-sauce"],["Oyster sauce","ingredient/oyster-sauce"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/01/Tulsi_or_Tulasi_Holy_basil.jpg/500px-Tulsi_or_Tulasi_Holy_basil.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Shashidhara halady","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tulsi_or_Tulasi_Holy_basil.jpg"}},"ingredient/cured-salmon":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cured-salmon","n":"Cured salmon","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Cured fish is fish which has been cured by subjecting it to fermentation, pickling, smoking, or some combination of these before it is eaten. These food preservation processes can include adding salt, nitrates, nitrite or sugar, can involve smoking and flavoring the fish, and may include cooking it. The earliest form of curing fish was dehydration. Other methods, such as smoking fish or salt-curing also go back for thousands of years. The term \"cure\" is derived from the Latin curare, meaning to take care of. It was first recorded in reference to fish in 1743."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cured-salmon"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cured_salmon"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["American Jewish cuisine","cuisine/american-jewish-cuisine",[["Lox","dish/lox"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/9c/North_carolina_algonkin-essen02.jpg/500px-North_carolina_algonkin-essen02.jpg","w":500,"h":337,"by":"John White","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:North_carolina_algonkin-essen02.jpg"}},"ingredient/pine-nut":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pine-nut","n":"Pine nut","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 5 dishes across 7 cuisines","intro":[["Pine nuts, also called piñón, pinoli (Italian: [piˈnɔːli]), or pignoli, are the edible seeds of pines (family Pinaceae, genus Pinus). According to the Food and Agriculture Organization, only 29 species provide edible nuts, while 20 are traded locally or internationally owing to their seed size being large enough to be worth harvesting; in other pines, the seeds are also edible but are too small to be of notable value as human food. The biggest exporters of pine nuts are China, Russia, North Korea, and Pakistan."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pine-nut"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pine_nut"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":7,"groups":[["Italia